---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const definedTermJsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "DefinedTerm",
  "@id": "https://drychillis.com/varieties/jinta/#term",
  "name": "Jinta Chilli",
  "description": "Medium-heat chilli from Gansu at 10,000–20,000 SHU. Long 11–14 cm pods with thick, glossy, deep jujube-red skin; used for visible chilli flakes, colour, seasoning and chilli paste.",
  "url": "https://drychillis.com/varieties/jinta/",
  "additionalProperty": [
    { "@type": "PropertyValue", "name": "Heat Level (SHU)", "value": "10,000 – 20,000" },
    { "@type": "PropertyValue", "name": "Region", "value": "Gansu" },
    { "@type": "PropertyValue", "name": "Fruit Type", "value": "Long pod (11–14 cm), thick glossy skin" },
    { "@type": "PropertyValue", "name": "Best For", "value": "Visible flakes, colour, seasoning" }
  ]
};

const faqJsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is Jinta chilli?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Jinta is a medium-heat chilli from Gansu at 10,000–20,000 SHU. Its pods are long (11–14 cm) with a deep jujube-red, thick glossy skin — a standard for visible flakes and bright colour. Colour and heat are independent, and Jinta leans colour."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How hot is Jinta pepper?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Jinta is medium heat, 10,000–20,000 SHU — mild enough for general seasoning and flakes, in the same band as Xian. It is chosen more for colour and appearance than for heat, which is why it is a flake variety first."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is Jinta pepper used for?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Jinta is used for visible chilli flakes, pizza topping, seasoning blends and chilli paste. Its deep red colour and thick glossy skin make it a preferred flake variety — but heat, colour and flavour vary independently, so name the variety to lock the colour profile."
      }
    }
  ]
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Jinta Chilli — 10,000–20,000 SHU | Tenda Peppers"
  description="Jinta pepper — medium-heat chilli from Gansu at 10,000–20,000 SHU, with long deep-red pods for visible flakes and bright colour. China manufacturer."
  jsonLd={[definedTermJsonLd, faqJsonLd]}
>
  <div class="container section">
    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" style="font-size:0.9rem;color:#6b5a54;margin-bottom:16px;">
      <a href="/">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/products/dried-chillies/">Dried Chillies</a> &rsaquo; Jinta
    </nav>
    <h1>Jinta Chilli</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      A medium-heat chilli at 10,000–20,000 SHU from Gansu, with long deep-jujube-red
      pods and a thick glossy skin — the standard for visible flakes and bright colour.
    </p>

    <div class="takeaways">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> Jinta is a medium-heat chilli (10,000–20,000 SHU) from Gansu, known for long jujube-red pods with thick, glossy skin. It is chosen for <em>visible</em> flakes and bright colour, not extreme heat — colour and heat are independent, and Jinta leans colour.
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">What is Jinta chilli?</h2>
    <p>
      Jinta is a medium-heat chilli chosen more for its appearance than its heat. The pods run
      11–14 cm long, about 2–2.4 cm across and roughly 17 g each, with a deep jujube-red colour and a
      thick, smooth, glossy skin. That combination makes it a preferred variety for flakes where the
      chilli must read clearly in the finished product. See the
      <a href="/heat-levels/medium-heat/">medium heat</a> page for the band.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Property</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Heat (SHU)</strong></td><td>10,000 – 20,000</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Region</strong></td><td>Gansu</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Fruit type</strong></td><td>Long pod (11–14 cm), thick glossy skin</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Best for</strong></td><td>Visible flakes, colour, seasoning</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">What is Jinta pepper used for?</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Visible chilli flakes</li>
      <li>Pizza topping and seasoning blends</li>
      <li>Chilli paste</li>
      <li>Whole dried chilli (medium heat)</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Jinta vs Erjingtiao vs Xian</h2>
    <p>
      Jinta and Xian sit in the same 10,000–20,000 SHU medium band, while Erjingtiao runs
      15,000–30,000 SHU. They are bought for different
      reasons. Jinta is the <strong>colour and appearance</strong> variety — long, deep-red, glossy
      pods that read clearly as visible flakes. Erjingtiao leans <strong>aroma</strong> and is the
      classic for chilli paste. Xian is the versatile middle — dark red and oily, used for chilli
      powder and sauce. If your product is judged by how the flake looks, choose Jinta; if it is
      judged by aroma, choose Erjingtiao.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">How to specify Jinta chilli</h2>
    <p>
      Name the variety, then the form and mesh. For flakes, 20–40 mesh keeps the red skin visible; for
      powder, 60–80 mesh. Confirm moisture (under 13%) and whether you need de-seeded flake. Jinta's
      colour is its selling point, so state your colour expectation (ASTA) if it matters.
    </p>
    <p class="data-note">
      Heat values are representative per variety. Confirm your exact SHU window, form and mesh when
      you inquire.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Jinta by the numbers: why it's a colour buy</h2>
    <p>
      Jinta pods run 11–14 cm long and roughly 17 g each — about <strong>59 pods to the kilo</strong>
      (1,000 g ÷ 17 g). That large, glossy pod is why Jinta reads clearly as a visible flake: each
      flake is a big, bright piece of red skin. On heat, Jinta is <strong>3.5–7× milder than
      Jixin</strong> (10,000–20,000 vs 50,000–70,000 SHU) — so it is bought for appearance and
      colour, not burn.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>What is Jinta chilli?</h3>
      <p>Jinta is a medium-heat chilli from Gansu at 10,000–20,000 SHU. Its pods are long (11–14 cm) with a deep jujube-red, thick glossy skin, making it a standard for visible flakes and bright colour.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>How hot is Jinta pepper?</h3>
      <p>Jinta is medium heat, 10,000–20,000 SHU — mild enough for general seasoning and flakes, with the heat of Xian. It is chosen more for colour and appearance than for heat.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>What is Jinta pepper used for?</h3>
      <p>Jinta is used for visible chilli flakes, pizza topping, seasoning blends and chilli paste. Its deep red colour and thick glossy skin make it a preferred flake variety.</p>
    </div>

    <div style="margin-top:32px;">
      <a class="cta" href="/contact/">Request Jinta Quotation</a>
      <a class="cta cta-outline" href="/products/dried-chillies/chilli-flakes/" style="margin-left:8px;">Chilli Flakes →</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
